      en: "The Anti-Lebanon Mountains, known as Jabal al-Sharqi, run parallel to the Lebanon Mountains along the Syrian-Lebanese border. The range reaches its highest point at Mount Hermon (2,814 m). It is composed mainly of Jurassic limestone. The mountains create a rain shadow, leaving the eastern slopes arid. They are sparsely populated, with nomadic herding in summer. The range contains the historic pass of Beït ed-Dine. It is also a habitat for the endangered Syrian wild ass. The mountains are a natural barrier between the coast and the interior.",
