      en: "The Syrian Coastal Mountain Range, also known as Jabal an-Nusayriyya, runs for 150 km parallel to the coast. Its highest peak, Nabi Yunis, reaches 1,562 meters. The mountains capture moisture from the Mediterranean, supporting dense forests of Aleppo pine and oak. They are the traditional homeland of the Alawite community. The slopes are terraced for olive, tobacco, and fruit cultivation. The range is a biodiversity hotspot, hosting the endangered Syrian brown bear. It also contains the ancient city of Ugarit's hinterland. Deforestation and overgrazing are environmental threats.",
