      en: "The Balikh River is a perennial river in northeastern Syria, originating from springs in Turkey and flowing 103 km to the Euphrates. Its drainage basin covers 14,400 km². The river is crucial for irrigating the Jazira plain, supplying over 200,000 ha of farmland. Historically, it marked borders between empires. Average discharge is 6 m³/s, varying seasonally. The Balikh hosts rare fish like the Mesopotamian catfish (Silurus triostegus). In recent decades, water levels have declined due to upstream dams and droughts, threatening biodiversity.",
